# Reviewed Gitleaks fingerprint suppressions.
#
# Register and rationale:
#   .github/RELEASE_SECURITY.md#secret-scanning-fingerprint-register
# Fail-closed validator:
#   .github/scripts/validate-secret-policy.py
#
# Each line is a Gitleaks fingerprint, `<commit>:<path>:<rule>:<line>`. A
# fingerprint pins one rule firing on one line of one file in one historical
# commit, so it is the narrowest suppression Gitleaks offers: it cannot hide the
# same value in any other commit, the same line under any other rule, or
# anything at all in the working tree. The same value reintroduced by a new
# commit is still reported, because that is a different commit and therefore a
# different fingerprint. This was verified, not assumed.
#
# This file exists because the full-history scan (`gitleaks git .`, run on push)
# reads commit patches, and history is immutable. A finding introduced by an old
# commit cannot be cleared by editing the value today: the old patch still
# contains it, and the new commit's own removal line is scanned too, which adds
# a second finding rather than removing the first. Rewriting published history
# is not an option, so a fingerprint is the only narrow remedy.
#
# Do not append to this file casually. The validator above rejects any entry
# that is not in its reviewed set and not documented in the register, so a new
# suppression fails the build until it has been reviewed and written down.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `generic-api-key` on the OPENPGPKEY row of the DNS record-type fixture table
# in `bc-dns-tools`. The table drives the record-type validation tests and lives
# inside `#[cfg(test)]`, next to the DNSKEY, DS, SMIMEA and TLSA fixtures.
#
# The value is a fabricated OpenPGP public-key blob. It decodes to the four
# bytes `99 02 0d 04` - the standard OpenPGP public-key packet header that makes
# every real key block start with `mQINB` - followed by the ASCII text
# `dandomKeyDataForTestingOnly`. It carries no key material, is not a
# credential, and never was one; it is a shape the OPENPGPKEY parser must
# accept. Upstream's `generic-api-key` fires on it purely because a base64 blob
# in quotes measures 4.7 bits of entropy.
#
# It is suppressed rather than rewritten because rewriting cannot work (see
# above), and it is not path-allowlisted because `import.rs` is production
# parser source: a path entry would blind every rule to that whole file forever.
#
# Two entries, one finding. `gitleaks git` (what CI runs) fingerprints with the
# commit; `gitleaks dir` fingerprints without one. Only the first is needed to
# make CI pass. The second keeps a local `gitleaks dir` run clean as well, so a
# developer is not met by a finding whose obvious fix - editing the value - is
# the one change that makes CI worse rather than better.
